The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
for (i = 0; i < priv->num_exprs; i++) {
    expr = nft_setelem_expr_at(elem_expr, elem_expr->size);
    if (nft_expr_clone(expr, priv->expr_array[i], GFP_ATOMIC) < 0)
        return -1;
    elem_expr->size += priv->expr_array[i]->ops->size;
}
```
Reason:
When `nft_expr_clone` fails for any expression after the first one (e.g., due to GFP_ATOMIC allocation failure), the function returns -1 immediately without freeing the previously cloned expressions that have already been added to `elem_expr`. This causes a memory leak of the first successfully cloned stateful expression, as shown in the reported backtrace.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
struct nft_ctx ctx = {
    .net	= read_pnet(&priv->set->net),
    .family	= priv->set->table->family,
};
...
for (i = 0; i < priv->num_exprs; i++) {
    expr = nft_setelem_expr_at(elem_expr, elem_expr->size);
    if (nft_expr_clone(expr, priv->expr_array[i], GFP_ATOMIC) < 0)
        goto err_out;
    elem_expr->size += priv->expr_array[i]->ops->size;
}
return 0;
err_out:
    nft_set_elem_expr_destroy(&ctx, elem_expr);
    return -1;
```
Reason:
The fix replaces the direct `return -1` with a `goto err_out` that calls `nft_set_elem_expr_destroy(&ctx, elem_expr)`. This function properly tears down all previously cloned expressions in `elem_expr`, including the first one, thus preventing the memory leak. The `ctx` structure is initialized with the necessary network namespace and family from the set. This ensures that any error during cloning of any expression (including the second or later ones) leads to cleanup of all already-set expressions before returning failure.

